Phase 1: Define C++ Data Layouts & API
To avoid per-frame allocations and interop overhead, pass pre-allocated memory buffers from C# into C++ rather than returning managed objects.
1. High-Performance Grid Layout
Represent a 2D/3D map using a flat 1D array (uint8_t* or int32_t*) rather than 2D/nested arrays (Tile**). This keeps memory contiguous and CPU cache-friendly.

Index=yWidth+x
2. Standard C-Compatible Export Interface
Export functions with C linkage (extern "C") to prevent C++ name mangling:
LevelGenerator.h
C++
#pragma once
#include <cstdint>

#if defined(_WIN32)
    #define EXPORT_API __declspec(dllexport)
#else
    #define EXPORT_API __attribute__((visibility("default")))
#endif

extern "C" {
    // Config struct passed across boundary
    struct LevelConfig {
        int32_t width;
        int32_t height;
        int32_t seed;
        int32_t minRoomSize;
        int32_t maxRooms;
    };

    // Generates map directly into pre-allocated buffer
    EXPORT_API void GenerateLevel(LevelConfig config, uint8_t* outMapData);
    
    // Optional query function for room bounding boxes or connectivity
    EXPORT_API int32_t GetRoomCount();
}

Phase 2: C++ Core Generation Engine
Choose an algorithm that fits your game style (e.g., Binary Space Partitioning (BSP), Cellular Automata, or Wave Function Collapse):
Memory Pooling: Pre-allocate room and corridor nodes in contiguous vectors/arrays to avoid runtime heap fragmentation.
Bitmasking & Fast Lookups: Use byte bitmasks for tile types (e.g., 0x00 = Empty, 0x01 = Wall, 0x02 = Floor, 0x04 = Door).
Multithreading (Optional Enhancement): Use std::async or OpenMP if generating multiple map layers or large noise heightmaps simultaneously.
Phase 3: Build Pipeline (CMake)
Set up a CMake script to output shared libraries (.dll on Windows, .so on Linux, .dylib on macOS) directly into Unity’s Assets/Plugins/ directory.
